+# tidyjson
+
+`tidyjson` reformats a JSON file and writes the result back to the same file.
+
+## Install
+
+From a checkout of this repository:
+
+```sh
+npm install -g .
+```
+
+This puts the `tidyjson` command on your `PATH`. It needs Node.js.
+
+## Usage
+
+```sh
+tidyjson <file> [--indent N] [--sort-keys]
+```
+
+| Option | Effect |
+| --- | --- |
+| `--indent N` | Indent nested values by `N` spaces. |
+| `--sort-keys` | Write object keys in sorted order. |
+
+Examples:
+
+```sh
+# Reformat with 4-space indentation
+tidyjson config.json --indent 4
+
+# Sort keys so diffs between versions stay stable
+tidyjson package-lock.json --sort-keys
+```
+
+`tidyjson` overwrites the file in place. Commit or copy the file first if you want to keep the original formatting.
+
+## Invalid JSON
+
+If the file does not parse as JSON, `tidyjson` exits with code 2 and leaves the file unchanged. A script can check for that code:
+
+```sh
+tidyjson data.json || echo "data.json is not valid JSON"
+```
+
+## Development
+
+Run the tests with:
+
+```sh
+npm test
+```
+
+This runs `node --test`.
